<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Arguin</h1><p class="st-meta">1445 CE – 1721 CE · capital Arguin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's first fortified trading post in Africa south of the Sahara, set up in 1445 on a small island off the Mauritanian coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portuguese expeditions sponsored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a> reached the Bay of Arguin in 1443. In 1445 a trading post was established on the island, and a fort was built around 1455, the first permanent European factory on the West African coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Arguin traded horses, cloth and grain to Saharan traders for gold, slaves and gum arabic, diverting part of the caravan trade to the sea. It was run by a royal monopoly and a garrison, with a chapel and a small community of traders, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> merchants came to the island to deal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took Arguin in 1633, and it then passed back and forth between the Dutch, English, French and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-african-company">Brandenburg African Company</a>, which held it from 1685. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took control in 1721. The fort was later abandoned, and the bay is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banc-darguin-national-park">Banc d'Arguin National Park</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arguin.jpg" alt="Arguin" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Vingboons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMH-6744-NA_View_of_Arguin_castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1445 CE – 1721 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt